/**
 * Glossary Batch 7 — New Domains
 * Covers: election law, tribal law, sports/entertainment, real estate/land use,
 *         securities (expanded), health law (expanded), military (expanded),
 *         maritime (expanded), education (expanded)
 * ~150 terms
 */
const terms = [

  // ─── ELECTION / VOTING LAW ───────────────────────────────────────────────
  ['Gerrymandering', 'The practice of drawing legislative district boundaries to give one political party an unfair electoral advantage by concentrating or dispersing opposition voters. "Packing" concentrates opposition voters into a single district to limit their influence elsewhere, while "cracking" splits concentrated opposition voter populations across multiple districts. The Supreme Court held in Rucho v. Common Cause (2019) that federal courts cannot review partisan gerrymandering claims, though racial gerrymandering remains subject to constitutional challenge under the Equal Protection Clause.', 'election', 'Federal/State', ['Redistricting', 'Racial Gerrymandering', 'Voter Dilution'], ['Rucho v. Common Cause (2019)', 'Voting Rights Act of 1965']],
  ['Voter Dilution', 'The dispersal or packing of minority voters in a way that diminishes their ability to elect representatives of their choice, in violation of the Equal Protection Clause or the Voting Rights Act. Section 2 of the Voting Rights Act prohibits voting practices that result in the denial or abridgement of minority voting rights. Courts apply the Gingles factors to determine whether vote dilution has occurred in a particular district.', 'election', 'Federal/State', ['Gerrymandering', 'Voting Rights Act', 'Redistricting'], ['Thornburg v. Gingles (1986)', '52 U.S.C. § 10301']],
  ['Citizens United Doctrine', 'The principle established in Citizens United v. Federal Election Commission (2010) that the First Amendment prohibits the government from restricting independent political expenditures by corporations, associations, or labor unions. The Court held that political speech does not lose First Amendment protection simply because its source is a corporation. This decision gave rise to the proliferation of Super PACs and dramatically altered campaign finance law.', 'election', 'Federal/State', ['Campaign Finance', 'Super PAC', 'First Amendment'], ['Citizens United v. FEC (2010)', 'Buckley v. Valeo (1976)']],
  ['Super PAC', 'An independent expenditure-only political action committee that may raise unlimited funds from corporations, unions, associations, and individuals and spend unlimited amounts on independent expenditures supporting or opposing political candidates. Super PACs cannot donate directly to or coordinate with candidates or party committees. They arose following the Citizens United (2010) and SpeechNow.org v. FEC (2010) decisions.', 'election', 'Federal/State', ['Citizens United Doctrine', 'Campaign Finance', 'FEC'], ['Citizens United v. FEC (2010)', '11 C.F.R. § 114.2']],
  ['Dark Money', 'Political spending by nonprofit organizations that are not required to disclose their donors, making it impossible for voters to know who is financing political advertising. These organizations, typically organized under IRC § 501(c)(4) as "social welfare" organizations or § 501(c)(6) as trade associations, can spend on political activities without disclosing contributors. Dark money spending has grown substantially since Citizens United.', 'election', 'Federal/State', ['Campaign Finance', 'Super PAC', 'Citizens United Doctrine'], ['IRC § 501(c)(4)', 'FEC regulations']],
  ['Preclearance', 'The requirement under Section 5 of the Voting Rights Act of 1965 that covered jurisdictions with histories of voting discrimination obtain federal approval before implementing any changes to voting laws or practices. The preclearance requirement applied to nine states and parts of others identified by a coverage formula in Section 4(b). The Supreme Court in Shelby County v. Holder (2013) struck down the coverage formula, rendering preclearance inoperative unless Congress enacts a new formula.', 'election', 'Federal/State', ['Voting Rights Act', 'Voter Dilution', 'Section 2 VRA'], ['Shelby County v. Holder (2013)', '52 U.S.C. § 10304']],
  ['Electoral College', 'The body of electors established by the Constitution to elect the President and Vice President, consisting of 538 electors with each state receiving the same number of electors as it has members of Congress. Presidential candidates typically receive all of a state\'s electoral votes under winner-take-all rules adopted by most states. Article II and the Twelfth Amendment govern the Electoral College, and a majority of 270 electoral votes is required to win the presidency.', 'election', 'Federal', ['Presidential Election', 'Faithless Elector', 'Popular Vote'], ['U.S. Const. art. II, § 1', 'U.S. Const. amend. XII']],
  ['Poll Tax', 'A fee required as a precondition for voting, historically used to disenfranchise African American and low-income voters in Southern states after Reconstruction. The Twenty-Fourth Amendment (1964) abolished poll taxes in federal elections, and in Harper v. Virginia Board of Elections (1966) the Supreme Court held that poll taxes in state elections also violate the Equal Protection Clause. Poll taxes represent a classic example of a facially neutral law with racially discriminatory purpose and effect.', 'election', 'Federal/State', ['Voter Suppression', 'Equal Protection', 'Voting Rights Act'], ['U.S. Const. amend. XXIV', 'Harper v. Virginia Board of Elections (1966)']],
  ['Redistricting', 'The process of redrawing the boundaries of legislative districts, typically conducted following each decennial census to account for population shifts. Federal law requires that congressional districts have substantially equal populations (Reynolds v. Sims, 1964). Redistricting may be conducted by state legislatures, independent commissions, or courts, and is subject to constitutional and statutory constraints including the prohibition on racial gerrymandering.', 'election', 'Federal/State', ['Gerrymandering', 'One Person One Vote', 'Voter Dilution'], ['Reynolds v. Sims (1964)', 'U.S. Const. art. I, § 2']],
  ['Ballot Initiative', 'A process by which citizens can place proposed legislation or constitutional amendments directly on the ballot for voter approval, bypassing the state legislature. About half the states permit some form of initiative. Proponents must collect a requisite number of signatures from registered voters to qualify a measure for the ballot. Direct democracy through ballot initiatives is subject to constitutional limitations, including the prohibition on denying equal protection.', 'election', 'State', ['Referendum', 'Recall Election', 'Direct Democracy'], ['Pacific Gas & Electric Co. v. State Energy Resources Conservation (1983)', 'State constitutions']],
  ['Felon Disenfranchisement', 'The practice of restricting or denying voting rights to individuals convicted of felony offenses. Laws vary widely by state: some states permanently disenfranchise felons, others restore rights upon release from prison, and a few (Maine and Vermont) allow incarcerated felons to vote. Section 2 of the Fourteenth Amendment suggests states may deny the right to vote for participation in crime. Approximately 6 million Americans are disenfranchised due to felony convictions.', 'election', 'State', ['Voting Rights', 'Criminal Disenfranchisement', 'Equal Protection'], ['Richardson v. Ramirez (1974)', 'U.S. Const. amend. XIV, § 2']],
  ['Motor Voter Act', 'The National Voter Registration Act of 1993 (NVRA), which requires states to offer voter registration opportunities when citizens apply for driver\'s licenses, public assistance, or at certain other government agencies. The Act also establishes uniform standards for voter registration list maintenance to prevent illegal purging. States must permit voter registration by mail and prohibit removal of registered voters solely due to non-voting.', 'election', 'Federal', ['Voter Registration', 'Voting Rights', 'HAVA'], ['52 U.S.C. § 20501 et seq.', 'NVRA']],
  ['Campaign Finance', 'The laws and regulations governing the raising and spending of money in political campaigns, designed to prevent corruption and the appearance of corruption in the electoral process. Federal campaign finance is primarily governed by the Federal Election Campaign Act (FECA) and the Bipartisan Campaign Reform Act (BCRA), enforced by the Federal Election Commission. Key concepts include contribution limits, expenditure limits, disclosure requirements, and the distinction between hard money and soft money.', 'election', 'Federal/State', ['Super PAC', 'Citizens United Doctrine', 'FEC'], ['52 U.S.C. § 30101 et seq.', 'Buckley v. Valeo (1976)']],
  ['Runoff Election', 'A second election held when no candidate in the primary or general election receives the required threshold of votes (typically a majority or plurality) to win. Runoff elections are common in Southern states and some municipal elections. Critics argue that runoff elections can disadvantage minority candidates and suppress turnout because they are held weeks after the initial election with lower voter participation.', 'election', 'State', ['Primary Election', 'Electoral College', 'Instant Runoff Voting'], ['State election laws', 'Voting Rights Act § 2']],
  ['Voter ID Law', 'Legislation requiring voters to present identification before casting a ballot, ranging from strict photo ID requirements to more flexible documentary alternatives. Proponents argue voter ID prevents fraud; opponents argue it burdens disadvantaged groups who disproportionately lack qualifying ID. The Supreme Court in Crawford v. Marion County Election Board (2008) upheld Indiana\'s photo ID law, but courts have struck down stricter laws as unconstitutionally burdensome.', 'election', 'State', ['Voter Suppression', 'Equal Protection', 'Voting Rights Act'], ['Crawford v. Marion County Election Board (2008)', 'Voting Rights Act of 1965']],
  ['Help America Vote Act (HAVA)', 'Federal legislation enacted in 2002 in response to voting irregularities in the 2000 presidential election that established minimum federal standards for elections, required provisional ballots, mandated accessible voting systems, and created the Election Assistance Commission (EAC). HAVA requires that states allow voters who claim to be registered to cast provisional ballots when their registration cannot be confirmed at the polls. It also required the replacement of punch-card and lever voting systems.', 'election', 'Federal', ['Provisional Ballot', 'Voting Rights', 'Motor Voter Act'], ['52 U.S.C. § 20901 et seq.', 'Help America Vote Act of 2002']],

  // ─── NATIVE AMERICAN / TRIBAL LAW ────────────────────────────────────────
  ['Tribal Sovereignty', 'The inherent right of federally recognized Indian tribes to govern their members and territories, derived from their status as distinct political communities predating the United States. Tribal sovereignty is not granted by Congress but rather retained by tribes as original sovereigns, subject to the plenary power of Congress to limit or extinguish it. Tribes possess powers of self-government including the right to determine membership, regulate internal affairs, and exercise civil and criminal jurisdiction over members.', 'tribal', 'Federal/Tribal', ['Federal Trust Responsibility', 'Indian Country', 'Plenary Power'], ['Worcester v. Georgia (1832)', 'Santa Clara Pueblo v. Martinez (1978)']],
  ['Federal Trust Responsibility', 'The United States government\'s special legal obligation to protect tribal lands, assets, resources, and treaty rights, arising from historical treaties and the unique relationship between the federal government and Indian tribes. Courts have recognized that this responsibility imposes enforceable duties on federal agencies in their dealings with tribes. The trust responsibility does not create a cause of action for damages absent a specific statutory duty, but it does inform judicial interpretation of ambiguous statutes affecting tribes.', 'tribal', 'Federal', ['Tribal Sovereignty', 'Indian Country', 'Treaty Rights'], ['Cobell v. Norton (D.D.C. 2001)', 'United States v. Mitchell (1983)']],
  ['Indian Country', 'The geographic territory subject to tribal and federal jurisdiction over Indian affairs, broadly defined by 18 U.S.C. § 1151 to include formal reservations, dependent Indian communities, and Indian allotments. Whether land constitutes Indian country determines which government — federal, state, or tribal — has jurisdiction over crimes, civil matters, and regulatory issues. The Supreme Court\'s landmark decision in McGirt v. Oklahoma (2020) confirmed that much of eastern Oklahoma constitutes Indian country as the historical Muscogee (Creek) Nation reservation.', 'tribal', 'Federal/Tribal', ['Tribal Sovereignty', 'Reservation', 'Major Crimes Act'], ['18 U.S.C. § 1151', 'McGirt v. Oklahoma (2020)']],
  ['Indian Child Welfare Act (ICWA)', 'A federal law enacted in 1978 that establishes minimum federal standards for the removal of Indian children from their families and placement in foster homes or for adoption, addressing historical practices of removing Indian children from their communities. ICWA grants tribal courts exclusive jurisdiction over custody proceedings involving tribal member children residing on the reservation and concurrent jurisdiction in other cases. Non-tribal courts must transfer cases to tribal court upon request absent good cause.', 'tribal', 'Federal', ['Tribal Sovereignty', 'Child Custody', 'Tribal Courts'], ['25 U.S.C. § 1901 et seq.', 'Brackeen v. Haaland (2023)']],
  ['Indian Gaming Regulatory Act (IGRA)', 'Federal legislation enacted in 1988 that establishes the regulatory framework for gaming activity on Indian lands, creating three classes of gaming with different regulatory requirements. Class I gaming (social/traditional games) is regulated solely by tribes; Class II gaming (bingo, pull-tabs) requires a tribal gaming ordinance approved by the National Indian Gaming Commission (NIGC); Class III gaming (casino-style) requires a tribal-state compact. States cannot refuse to negotiate Class III gaming compacts in good faith.', 'tribal', 'Federal', ['Tribal Sovereignty', 'Tribal Gaming Compact', 'NIGC'], ['25 U.S.C. § 2701 et seq.', 'California v. Cabazon Band of Mission Indians (1987)']],
  ['Major Crimes Act', 'A federal law providing that certain serious crimes committed by Indians in Indian country are subject to federal prosecution, regardless of tribal law. The Act was enacted in 1885 following Ex parte Crow Dog (1883), in which the Supreme Court held the federal government lacked jurisdiction over an Indian who murdered another Indian on the reservation. Currently lists thirteen enumerated felonies including murder, manslaughter, kidnapping, sexual abuse, and robbery.', 'tribal', 'Federal', ['Indian Country', 'Tribal Courts', 'Federal Jurisdiction'], ['18 U.S.C. § 1153', 'Ex parte Crow Dog (1883)']],
  ['Tribal Courts', 'Judicial institutions established by federally recognized tribes to adjudicate matters arising under tribal law and exercise jurisdiction over matters occurring in Indian country. Tribal courts have inherent civil jurisdiction over tribal members and, in limited circumstances, over non-members. In Oliphant v. Suquamish Indian Tribe (1978), the Supreme Court held that tribes lack inherent criminal jurisdiction over non-Indians, though Congress partially restored this through the Violence Against Women Act (2022).', 'tribal', 'Tribal', ['Tribal Sovereignty', 'Indian Country', 'Major Crimes Act'], ['Oliphant v. Suquamish Indian Tribe (1978)', 'VAWA 2022 Reauthorization']],
  ['Treaty Rights', 'Rights retained by Indian tribes through solemn agreements with the United States, including rights to hunt, fish, gather, and use lands in territories ceded by treaty. Courts construe ambiguous treaty language in favor of the tribe (the "Indian canon of construction") and recognize that tribes retain rights not expressly surrendered. In United States v. Winans (1905) and subsequent cases, the Supreme Court has broadly protected off-reservation treaty rights against state interference.', 'tribal', 'Federal/Tribal', ['Tribal Sovereignty', 'Federal Trust Responsibility', 'Off-Reservation Rights'], ['United States v. Winans (1905)', 'Washington v. Washington State Commercial Passenger Fishing Vessel Assn. (1979)']],
  ['Plenary Power (Indian Law)', 'The constitutional authority of Congress to regulate all aspects of Indian affairs, including the power to abrogate treaty rights, dissolve tribal governments, and terminate the federal-tribal relationship. The plenary power doctrine traces to Lone Wolf v. Hitchcock (1903), where the Supreme Court deferred to Congress on Indian affairs. While the doctrine remains nominally intact, modern courts have limited its application and required clearer statements before finding that Congress intended to abrogate Indian rights.', 'tribal', 'Federal', ['Tribal Sovereignty', 'Treaty Rights', 'Federal Trust Responsibility'], ['Lone Wolf v. Hitchcock (1903)', 'United States v. Sioux Nation of Indians (1980)']],
  ['Native American Graves Protection and Repatriation Act (NAGPRA)', 'A federal law enacted in 1990 requiring museums and federal agencies to consult with affiliated Indian tribes and Native Hawaiian organizations and to repatriate human remains, funerary objects, sacred objects, and objects of cultural patrimony upon request. Institutions are required to inventory their collections, determine cultural affiliation, and notify tribes of relevant holdings. Criminal penalties apply for illegal trafficking in Native American cultural items.', 'tribal', 'Federal', ['Cultural Property', 'Indigenous Rights', 'Federal Agencies'], ['25 U.S.C. § 3001 et seq.', 'NAGPRA Final Rule (2024)']],
  ['Allotment', 'The federal policy implemented primarily through the Dawes Act of 1887 that divided communally held tribal lands into individual parcels allocated to tribal members, with remaining "surplus" lands sold to non-Indians. Allotment resulted in the loss of approximately 90 million acres of Indian land and significantly undermined tribal sovereignty. The Indian Reorganization Act of 1934 ended further allotment and partially restored tribal self-governance.', 'tribal', 'Federal', ['Indian Country', 'Tribal Lands', 'Federal Trust Responsibility'], ['Dawes Act of 1887', 'Indian Reorganization Act of 1934']],
  ['Bureau of Indian Affairs (BIA)', 'The federal agency within the Department of the Interior responsible for administering and managing 55 million acres of land held in trust for American Indians and Alaska Natives and for fulfilling the federal government\'s trust responsibilities to tribal nations. The BIA administers federal programs relating to education, social services, law enforcement, and economic development on reservations. BIA decisions are subject to judicial review under the Administrative Procedure Act and the federal trust responsibility.', 'tribal', 'Federal', ['Federal Trust Responsibility', 'Tribal Sovereignty', 'Indian Country'], ['25 U.S.C. § 1 et seq.', 'Cobell v. Salazar (2009)']],
  ['Sovereignty Immunity (Tribal)', 'The doctrine that Indian tribes, as sovereigns, are immune from suit in federal, state, and tribal courts unless Congress has clearly and expressly abrogated that immunity or the tribe itself has clearly waived it. Tribal immunity extends to tribal enterprises and governmental entities. The Supreme Court has recently limited tribal immunity, holding in Michigan v. Bay Mills Indian Community (2014) that it extends to off-reservation commercial activity but noting congressional authority to abrogate.', 'tribal', 'Federal/Tribal', ['Tribal Sovereignty', 'Sovereign Immunity', 'Indian Country'], ['Michigan v. Bay Mills Indian Community (2014)', 'Santa Clara Pueblo v. Martinez (1978)']],

  // ─── SPORTS & ENTERTAINMENT LAW ─────────────────────────────────────────
  ['Right of Publicity', 'The right of an individual to control the commercial use of their name, image, likeness, and other aspects of personal identity. It is primarily a state law right, with about half of U.S. states recognizing it by statute and others through common law. The right may survive death (allowing estates to control the deceased celebrity\'s likeness) and can be assigned and licensed. Violations occur when a defendant commercially uses a living or deceased person\'s identity without consent.', 'sports-entertainment', 'State', ['Name Image Likeness', 'Privacy', 'Trademark'], ['Zacchini v. Scripps-Howard Broadcasting Co. (1977)', 'California Civil Code § 3344']],
  ['Name, Image, and Likeness (NIL)', 'The right of college and amateur athletes to profit from the commercial use of their name, image, and likeness, recognized following the NCAA\'s 2021 rule change and subsequent state laws prompting it. Before 2021, NCAA rules prohibited student-athletes from receiving any compensation for their NIL as a condition of amateur eligibility. The Supreme Court\'s unanimous decision in NCAA v. Alston (2021) under antitrust law, combined with multiple state NIL statutes, effectively ended NCAA amateurism restrictions.', 'sports-entertainment', 'Federal/State', ['Right of Publicity', 'NCAA Regulations', 'Antitrust Law'], ['NCAA v. Alston (2021)', 'California Education Code § 67456']],
  ['Sports Collective Bargaining Agreement (CBA)', 'The contract between a professional sports league or team owners and the players\' union that governs wages, hours, working conditions, and related labor terms for professional athletes. CBAs in professional sports set minimum salaries, revenue sharing formulas, draft rules, free agency conditions, and grievance procedures. Federal labor law (NLRA) governs the negotiation process and protects the right to collectively bargain; the non-statutory labor exemption shields CBA provisions from antitrust challenge.', 'sports-entertainment', 'Federal', ['Collective Bargaining', 'NLRA', 'Antitrust Law'], ['National Labor Relations Act', 'Brown v. Pro Football Inc. (1996)']],
  ['Reserve Clause', 'A contractual provision in professional sports contracts that historically bound a player to their team even after the contract expired, effectively granting the team perpetual rights to the player\'s services. The reserve clause was the subject of Curt Flood\'s challenge in Flood v. Kuhn (1972), where the Supreme Court reaffirmed baseball\'s antitrust exemption. Arbitrator Peter Seitz\'s 1975 decision in the Andy Messersmith case effectively eliminated the reserve clause in Major League Baseball, establishing modern free agency.', 'sports-entertainment', 'Federal', ['Free Agency', 'Antitrust Law', 'Sports CBA'], ['Flood v. Kuhn (1972)', 'Messersmith/McNally arbitration (1975)']],
  ['Sports Arbitration', 'A private dispute resolution mechanism used in professional sports to resolve contract disputes, disciplinary matters, salary disputes, and grievances between players and clubs outside the court system. Arbitration clauses in CBAs and player contracts require binding arbitration before a neutral arbitrator. Courts apply the Federal Arbitration Act (FAA) and give deference to arbitral awards in sports cases, as illustrated by the Tom Brady "Deflategate" case.', 'sports-entertainment', 'Federal', ['CBA', 'Grievance Procedure', 'Federal Arbitration Act'], ['Federal Arbitration Act', 'NFL v. Brady (2d Cir. 2016)']],
  ['Anti-Doping Regulations', 'Rules and enforcement procedures governing the prohibition of performance-enhancing drugs in professional and amateur sports, administered in the U.S. primarily by the United States Anti-Doping Agency (USADA) under the World Anti-Doping Code (WADC). Athletes are subject to out-of-competition testing and strict liability for prohibited substances found in their samples, regardless of how the substance entered their system. Sanctions range from a reprimand to a four-year or lifetime ban depending on the substance and circumstances.', 'sports-entertainment', 'Federal/International', ['WADA', 'USADA', 'Sports Arbitration'], ['World Anti-Doping Code', '21 U.S.C. § 2006 (Ted Stevens Act)']],
  ['Entertainment Option Contract', 'A contract in the entertainment industry granting one party the exclusive right to acquire a property (screenplay, novel, life rights) for development within a specified period, typically 12-18 months, for an agreed option price. If the option is exercised, a larger purchase price applies for the underlying rights. Options are renewable, usually at an increased price, and protect studios and producers while the property is being developed.', 'sports-entertainment', 'Federal/State', ['Work for Hire', 'Copyright Assignment', 'Development Deal'], ['Copyright Act', 'WGA Minimum Basic Agreement']],
  ['Work-for-Hire (Entertainment)', 'A doctrine under copyright law providing that copyrights in works created by employees within the scope of their employment, or in certain specially commissioned works under a written agreement, vest in the employer or commissioning party rather than the actual creator. In the entertainment industry, studios structure production deals as work-for-hire to own all intellectual property created. The Copyright Act\'s termination rights (§§ 203, 304) cannot be contractually waived but apply differently to works-for-hire.', 'sports-entertainment', 'Federal', ['Copyright', 'Copyright Assignment', 'Termination Rights'], ['17 U.S.C. § 101', '17 U.S.C. § 203']],
  ['Talent Agency Regulation', 'State laws governing licensed talent agents who negotiate contracts on behalf of actors, musicians, athletes, and other performers for a commission, typically regulated by states like California under the Talent Agencies Act. Unlicensed procurement of employment contracts may render the agreements voidable at the talent\'s election. Managers, unlike agents, are generally not required to be licensed but are prohibited from directly procuring employment under California law.', 'sports-entertainment', 'State', ['Right of Publicity', 'Contract', 'Entertainment Contract'], ['California Labor Code § 1700 et seq.', 'California Talent Agencies Act']],
  ['Defamation in Entertainment (SLAPP)', 'Strategic Lawsuits Against Public Participation (SLAPP) claims and celebrity defamation suits are frequently litigated in the entertainment context where public figures must prove actual malice under the New York Times v. Sullivan standard. Anti-SLAPP statutes in approximately 30 states allow defendants to seek early dismissal of suits brought primarily to silence criticism or commentary. California\'s robust anti-SLAPP statute (CCP § 425.16) is frequently invoked in entertainment and media litigation.', 'sports-entertainment', 'Federal/State', ['Defamation', 'First Amendment', 'Public Figure'], ['New York Times v. Sullivan (1964)', 'Cal. CCP § 425.16']],

  // ─── REAL ESTATE / LAND USE ──────────────────────────────────────────────
  ['Zoning', 'A system of land use regulation that divides a municipality into districts (zones) and prescribes the types of uses permitted in each zone (residential, commercial, industrial, mixed-use). Zoning is a police power exercise by local governments and must bear a substantial relationship to public health, safety, morals, or general welfare. The Supreme Court in Euclid v. Ambler Realty (1926) upheld comprehensive zoning ordinances as constitutional.', 'real-estate', 'State/Local', ['Variance', 'Non-Conforming Use', 'Special Use Permit'], ['Euclid v. Ambler Realty Co. (1926)', 'Standard Zoning Enabling Act']],
  ['Variance', 'An exception to the strict application of a zoning ordinance granted by a zoning board of appeals or adjustment when strict compliance would result in unnecessary hardship. An area variance (dimensional relief) differs from a use variance (permission for a use not allowed in the zone). Applicants must typically show unique hardship resulting from the property\'s physical characteristics, not personal or financial hardship, and that the variance will not harm the neighborhood.', 'real-estate', 'State/Local', ['Zoning', 'Special Use Permit', 'Hardship'], ['Standard Zoning Enabling Act', 'Zoning Board of Appeals']],
  ['Eminent Domain', 'The power of federal, state, and local governments to take private property for public use, conditioned upon payment of just compensation. The Fifth Amendment\'s Takings Clause provides: "nor shall private property be taken for public use, without just compensation." Eminent domain may be exercised directly by government or delegated to private entities like utilities and railroads. The Supreme Court\'s controversial Kelo v. City of New London (2005) decision upheld taking private property for economic development.', 'real-estate', 'Federal/State', ['Just Compensation', 'Regulatory Taking', 'Kelo v. New London'], ['U.S. Const. amend. V', 'Kelo v. City of New London (2005)']],
  ['Just Compensation', 'The fair market value of property taken through eminent domain, representing what a willing buyer would pay a willing seller in an arm\'s-length transaction. Just compensation does not include sentimental value, business losses, or moving expenses unless separately required by statute. When a property owner disputes the government\'s valuation, a condemnation proceeding is held in which a jury typically determines the amount.', 'real-estate', 'Federal/State', ['Eminent Domain', 'Fair Market Value', 'Regulatory Taking'], ['U.S. Const. amend. V', 'United States v. 564.54 Acres of Monroe and Pike County Land (1979)']],
  ['Regulatory Taking', 'A government regulation that goes so far in restricting property use that it is effectively a taking requiring just compensation under the Fifth Amendment, even though the government has not physically seized the property. The Penn Central Transportation Co. v. New York City (1978) balancing test considers economic impact on the owner, reasonable investment-backed expectations, and the character of the government action. A categorical taking occurs when regulation eliminates all economically beneficial use (Lucas v. South Carolina Coastal Council, 1992).', 'real-estate', 'Federal/State', ['Eminent Domain', 'Zoning', 'Fifth Amendment'], ['Penn Central Transportation Co. v. N.Y.C. (1978)', 'Lucas v. South Carolina Coastal Council (1992)']],
  ['Easement Appurtenant', 'An easement that benefits a particular parcel of land (dominant tenement) by burdening another parcel (servient tenement), creating a property right that runs with the land and transfers automatically with conveyance of the dominant estate. A common example is a right-of-way across one parcel to reach a landlocked parcel. Both the benefit and burden are attached to the land and pass to successive owners through deed.', 'real-estate', 'State', ['Easement in Gross', 'Prescriptive Easement', 'Servient Tenement'], ['Restatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes', 'Black\'s Law Dictionary']],
  ['Prescriptive Easement', 'An easement acquired by using another\'s land for a period meeting the statutory limitations period in an open, notorious, hostile, and continuous manner, similar to adverse possession but creating only a right of use rather than title. The use must be adverse to the owner\'s interest, not permissive. Many states require the use to be under a claim of right. Prescriptive easements commonly arise from paths or roads used by neighbors over time.', 'real-estate', 'State', ['Adverse Possession', 'Easement Appurtenant', 'Statute of Limitations'], ['Restatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes', 'State real property statutes']],
  ['Restrictive Covenant / CC&Rs', 'Limitations on the use of land contained in deeds or declarations (Covenants, Conditions, and Restrictions) that restrict how property owners may use their land. Restrictive covenants run with the land and bind successive owners if they are in writing, the original parties intended it to run, and the restriction touches and concerns the land. Racially restrictive covenants are unenforceable following Shelley v. Kraemer (1948). Modern CC&Rs govern planned communities and homeowners associations.', 'real-estate', 'State', ['Homeowners Association', 'Easement', 'Servient Tenement'], ['Shelley v. Kraemer (1948)', 'Restatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes']],
  ['Title Insurance', 'Insurance protecting real property owners and mortgage lenders against losses arising from defects in title, including forgery, undisclosed heirs, recording errors, liens, and boundary disputes. An owner\'s policy protects the buyer; a lender\'s policy protects the mortgage lender. Title insurance is distinguished from other insurance in that it insures against past events at the time of purchase rather than future risks. Title companies conduct a title search before issuing a policy.', 'real-estate', 'State', ['Chain of Title', 'Title Defect', 'Recording Acts'], ['RESPA', 'State title insurance statutes']],
  ['Quiet Title Action', 'A lawsuit to establish clear ownership of real property by resolving competing claims to title, adjudicating boundary disputes, eliminating clouds on title, and establishing the rights of all claimants. The plaintiff must have a current interest in the property. Courts resolve quiet title actions by declaring the rights of all parties with respect to the property. A judgment quieting title is recorded in the county recorder\'s office to provide constructive notice of the determination.', 'real-estate', 'State', ['Title Insurance', 'Adverse Possession', 'Chain of Title'], ['State code of civil procedure', 'Black\'s Law Dictionary']],
  ['Recording Acts', 'State statutes governing the recording of real property instruments (deeds, mortgages, liens) that protect subsequent purchasers from prior unrecorded interests. Under a "race" statute, the first to record wins regardless of notice. Under a "notice" statute, a subsequent purchaser without notice of a prior unrecorded interest prevails. Under a "race-notice" statute (most common), a subsequent purchaser prevails only if they recorded first AND had no prior notice of the unrecorded claim.', 'real-estate', 'State', ['Chain of Title', 'Title Insurance', 'Lis Pendens'], ['State recording statutes', 'Restatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages']],
  ['Lis Pendens', 'A notice recorded in the public land records indicating that a lawsuit affecting a specific piece of real property is pending, providing constructive notice to subsequent purchasers and lenders that their interests may be subject to the outcome of the litigation. From the Latin "suit pending," a lis pendens is filed in the county recorder\'s office where the property is located. It does not constitute a lien but serves as notice, effectively clouding title until the litigation is resolved.', 'real-estate', 'State', ['Quiet Title Action', 'Recording Acts', 'Title Insurance'], ['State civil procedure codes', 'Black\'s Law Dictionary']],
  ['Deed of Trust vs. Mortgage', 'Two instruments used to create a security interest in real property as collateral for a loan. A mortgage creates a two-party relationship between borrower (mortgagor) and lender (mortgagee), while a deed of trust involves three parties — borrower (trustor), lender (beneficiary), and a neutral trustee holding title pending repayment. Deeds of trust are common in western states and allow non-judicial foreclosure through a power of sale, which is typically faster than judicial mortgage foreclosure.', 'real-estate', 'State', ['Foreclosure', 'Security Interest', 'Title Insurance'], ['State property law', 'Restatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages']],
  ['RESPA (Real Estate Settlement Procedures Act)', 'A federal consumer protection law requiring disclosures of settlement costs in residential real estate transactions and prohibiting kickbacks and referral fees among settlement service providers. RESPA applies to federally related mortgage loans and requires lenders to provide a Loan Estimate and Closing Disclosure to borrowers. The Consumer Financial Protection Bureau (CFPB) enforces RESPA and may impose civil penalties for violations including undisclosed kickbacks between title companies, attorneys, and real estate agents.', 'real-estate', 'Federal', ['Title Insurance', 'Mortgage', 'CFPB'], ['12 U.S.C. § 2601 et seq.', 'CFPB RESPA regulations']],
  ['Adverse Possession', 'A doctrine by which a person who possesses another\'s land under certain conditions for the statutory period (varying by state, typically 5-21 years) can acquire legal title to that land without compensation to the original owner. Elements typically include actual, open, notorious, exclusive, continuous, and hostile possession under a claim of right. Adverse possession serves the policy of rewarding productive use of land and clearing uncertain title.', 'real-estate', 'State', ['Prescriptive Easement', 'Quiet Title Action', 'Statute of Limitations'], ['Restatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes', 'State real property statutes']],

  // ─── SECURITIES LAW (EXPANDED) ───────────────────────────────────────────
  ['Rule 10b-5', 'The primary anti-fraud provision in securities law, promulgated by the SEC under Section 10(b) of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934, prohibiting any person from making false statements, omitting material facts, or engaging in any scheme to defraud in connection with the purchase or sale of any security. Private plaintiffs suing under Rule 10b-5 must prove: a material misrepresentation or omission, scienter, a connection with the purchase or sale of securities, reliance, economic loss, and loss causation. The implied private right of action was recognized in Superintendent of Insurance v. Bankers Life (1971).', 'securities', 'Federal', ['Securities Fraud', 'Material Non-Public Information', 'SEC Enforcement'], ['17 C.F.R. § 240.10b-5', 'Basic Inc. v. Levinson (1988)']],
  ['Insider Trading', 'Trading in a company\'s securities by a person who possesses material non-public information (MNPI) in breach of a duty of trust and confidence owed to the issuer, its shareholders, or the source of the information. The classical theory applies to corporate insiders trading on their company\'s information; the misappropriation theory (United States v. O\'Hagan, 1997) extends liability to outsiders who misappropriate information in breach of a duty to the source. Penalties include disgorgement, civil fines up to three times the profit, and criminal imprisonment up to 20 years.', 'securities', 'Federal', ['Rule 10b-5', 'Material Non-Public Information', 'SEC Enforcement'], ['15 U.S.C. § 78j(b)', 'United States v. O\'Hagan (1997)']],
  ['Initial Public Offering (IPO)', 'The first time a company offers its securities to the public through a registered offering under the Securities Act of 1933, transforming it from a private to a public company subject to ongoing SEC reporting obligations. The IPO process involves selecting underwriters, conducting due diligence, filing a registration statement (Form S-1) with the SEC, and a "road show" to market shares to institutional investors. Companies must satisfy Exchange Act reporting requirements (Form 10-K, 10-Q, 8-K) following the IPO.', 'securities', 'Federal', ['Securities Registration', 'SEC Disclosure', 'Underwriting'], ['15 U.S.C. § 77a et seq.', 'SEC Regulation S-K']],
  ['Sarbanes-Oxley Act (SOX)', 'The Sarbanes-Oxley Act of 2002, enacted in response to the Enron and WorldCom accounting scandals, that established enhanced standards for public company boards, management, and public accounting firms. Key provisions include CEO/CFO certification of financial statements (Sections 302 and 906), prohibition on officer and director loans, whistleblower protections, enhanced civil and criminal penalties for securities fraud, and creation of the Public Company Accounting Oversight Board (PCAOB). Section 404 requires annual assessment of internal controls over financial reporting.', 'securities', 'Federal', ['SEC Disclosure', 'Auditing Standards', 'Corporate Governance'], ['15 U.S.C. § 7201 et seq.', 'Sarbanes-Oxley Act of 2002']],
  ['Proxy Statement', 'A document required by SEC rules to be sent to shareholders before a vote on matters requiring shareholder approval, providing information on proposals, director nominees, executive compensation, and related party transactions. The SEC proxy rules (Regulation 14A) govern the content and timing of proxy solicitations. Contested proxy solicitations (proxy fights) occur when dissident shareholders mount campaigns to elect alternative directors or defeat management proposals.', 'securities', 'Federal', ['Shareholder Rights', 'Corporate Governance', 'SEC Disclosure'], ['15 U.S.C. § 78n', 'SEC Schedule 14A']],
  ['Short-Swing Profits (Section 16(b))', 'A provision of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934 that requires directors, officers, and shareholders owning more than 10% of a class of equity securities to disgorge any profits from "short-swing" transactions — the purchase and sale (or sale and purchase) of the company\'s equity securities within a six-month period. The provision creates strict liability regardless of the insider\'s use of material non-public information. The issuer (or a shareholder on its behalf) may sue to recover disgorgement without proving any wrongdoing beyond the timing of trades.', 'securities', 'Federal', ['Insider Trading', 'Corporate Governance', 'SEC Reporting'], ['15 U.S.C. § 78p(b)', 'Section 16 reporting rules']],
  ['Tender Offer', 'A public offer to shareholders of a company to purchase their shares at a specified price (typically at a premium to market) during a limited time, often made in connection with a hostile or negotiated acquisition. The Williams Act amendments to the Securities Exchange Act regulate tender offers, requiring disclosure, a minimum acceptance period, withdrawal rights, and pro-rata purchase rules. Response strategies available to target companies include poison pills, white knight defenses, and board-level defensive measures.', 'securities', 'Federal', ['Merger & Acquisition', 'Proxy Statement', 'Corporate Governance'], ['15 U.S.C. § 78m(d)-(e)', 'Williams Act (1968)']],
  ['Blue Sky Laws', 'State securities laws enacted to protect investors from speculative or fraudulent investment schemes, operating parallel to federal securities laws. The term derives from an early description of promoters who would sell shares of nothing more than "blue sky." Most states require registration of securities offerings, broker-dealers, and investment advisers. The National Securities Markets Improvement Act (NSMIA) of 1996 preempts state registration requirements for federally covered securities, limiting state oversight to broker-dealers.', 'securities', 'State', ['Securities Registration', 'Broker-Dealer', 'FINRA'], ['Hall v. Geiger-Jones (1917)', 'State securities statutes']],
  ['FINRA', 'The Financial Industry Regulatory Authority, a non-governmental self-regulatory organization that regulates broker-dealers and their registered representatives in the United States. FINRA is authorized by Congress and overseen by the SEC to write and enforce rules governing broker-dealer conduct, examine firms for compliance, discipline rule violators, and operate a dispute resolution forum for investor claims. FINRA arbitration is mandatory for most customer disputes against broker-dealers under standard brokerage account agreements.', 'securities', 'Federal', ['Broker-Dealer', 'Securities Regulation', 'Arbitration'], ['Securities Exchange Act § 15A', '15 U.S.C. § 78o-3']],

  // ─── HEALTH LAW (EXPANDED) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
  ['EMTALA', 'The Emergency Medical Treatment and Active Labor Act (1986), a federal law requiring all hospitals participating in Medicare that operate emergency departments to provide medical screening examinations and necessary stabilizing treatment to any patient presenting with an emergency medical condition, regardless of ability to pay. EMTALA applies to the actively laboring patient, requiring hospitals to stabilize or appropriately transfer. Hospitals violating EMTALA may face civil monetary penalties, exclusion from Medicare, and private civil liability.', 'health', 'Federal', ['Medical Malpractice', 'Medicare', 'Access to Care'], ['42 U.S.C. § 1395dd', 'CMS EMTALA Interpretive Guidelines']],
  ['Stark Law', 'The federal physician self-referral law (42 U.S.C. § 1395nn) that prohibits physicians from referring Medicare patients for designated health services to entities in which the physician or their immediate family member has a financial relationship, unless a statutory or regulatory exception applies. Stark Law is a strict liability statute — no intent to violate the law is required. Penalties include denial of payment, mandatory refund of payments received, civil monetary penalties up to $15,000 per violation, and exclusion from federal health care programs.', 'health', 'Federal', ['Anti-Kickback Statute', 'Healthcare Fraud', 'False Claims Act'], ['42 U.S.C. § 1395nn', 'CMS Stark Law regulations']],
  ['Anti-Kickback Statute (Healthcare)', 'A federal criminal statute (42 U.S.C. § 1320a-7b) that prohibits knowingly and willfully offering, paying, soliciting, or receiving anything of value to induce or reward referrals of items or services covered by federal health care programs. Unlike the Stark Law, the Anti-Kickback Statute requires intent and applies beyond physician self-referrals to all participants in federal health care programs. Safe harbors protect certain legitimate business arrangements such as employment, personal services, and group purchasing organizations.', 'health', 'Federal', ['Stark Law', 'Healthcare Fraud', 'False Claims Act'], ['42 U.S.C. § 1320a-7b', 'OIG Safe Harbor regulations']],
  ['False Claims Act (Healthcare)', 'A federal law (31 U.S.C. § 3729) that imposes civil liability on persons who knowingly submit false or fraudulent claims for payment to the government. In healthcare, the FCA is the principal tool for combating Medicare and Medicaid fraud, encompassing false billing, upcoding, unbundling, and billing for services not rendered. Qui tam provisions allow private relators (whistleblowers) to file suit on behalf of the government and receive a share (15-30%) of any recovery. Treble damages and penalties of $13,000-$27,000 per false claim may be assessed.', 'health', 'Federal', ['Stark Law', 'Anti-Kickback Statute', 'Medicaid Fraud'], ['31 U.S.C. § 3729 et seq.', 'U.S. ex rel. Woodruff v. Hawaii Pacific Health (2010)']],
  ['Informed Consent (Medical)', 'The legal and ethical doctrine requiring physicians to disclose to patients the risks, benefits, alternatives, and nature of a proposed treatment so that patients can make a voluntary and knowledgeable decision about their care. Two standards govern the scope of required disclosure: the professional standard (what a reasonable physician would disclose) and the patient standard (what a reasonable patient would want to know). Lack of informed consent is an independent basis for medical malpractice liability separate from negligent treatment.', 'health', 'State', ['Medical Malpractice', 'Patient Rights', 'Battery'], ['Canterbury v. Spence (1972)', 'Restatement (Third) of Torts: Medical Malpractice']],
  ['Affordable Care Act (ACA)', 'The Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act of 2010, which made comprehensive reforms to the U.S. health insurance system including the prohibition on denying coverage based on pre-existing conditions, the mandate for essential health benefits, expansion of Medicaid, creation of health insurance exchanges, and the individual mandate (upheld in NFIB v. Sebelius, 2012, as a tax). The ACA\'s constitutionality and scope have been litigated extensively, with the Supreme Court preserving its central provisions while striking down mandatory Medicaid expansion.', 'health', 'Federal', ['Medicaid', 'Medicare', 'Health Insurance'], ['42 U.S.C. § 18001 et seq.', 'NFIB v. Sebelius (2012)']],
  ['Mental Health Parity Act', 'The Mental Health Parity and Addiction Equity Act of 2008 (MHPAEA), requiring health insurers and group health plans that provide mental health and substance use disorder benefits to provide them on terms no more restrictive than coverage for medical and surgical conditions. Parity applies to financial requirements (copays, deductibles), quantitative treatment limitations (day and visit limits), and non-quantitative treatment limitations (prior authorization policies). The Departments of Labor, HHS, and Treasury share enforcement authority over MHPAEA.', 'health', 'Federal', ['Health Insurance', 'Disability Rights', 'ACA'], ['29 U.S.C. § 1185a', 'MHPAEA (2008)']],
  ['Telemedicine Law', 'The body of laws and regulations governing the delivery of healthcare services through electronic communications technologies including video, audio, and digital data transmission. Telemedicine regulation involves medical licensure (physicians must typically be licensed in the patient\'s state), prescribing authority, reimbursement (Medicare, Medicaid, and private insurer coverage varies), and privacy compliance (HIPAA). Following COVID-19, states and the federal government significantly expanded telemedicine access and reimbursement.', 'health', 'Federal/State', ['HIPAA', 'Medical Licensing', 'Medicare'], ['State medical practice acts', '42 C.F.R. § 410.78']],

  // ─── MILITARY / VETERANS LAW (EXPANDED) ─────────────────────────────────
  ['Non-Judicial Punishment (Article 15)', 'A disciplinary measure available to commanding officers under Article 15 of the Uniform Code of Military Justice (UCMJ) to address minor offenses without resort to a court-martial, providing a quicker and less formal alternative. The service member may refuse NJP and demand trial by court-martial instead, except when attached to a vessel at sea. Punishment may include reduction in grade, forfeiture of pay, extra duties, and restriction to limits.', 'military', 'Federal/Military', ['Court-Martial', 'UCMJ', 'Military Discipline'], ['UCMJ Art. 15', '10 U.S.C. § 815']],
  ['Court-Martial', 'A military tribunal convened to try members of the armed forces for violations of the Uniform Code of Military Justice (UCMJ). There are three types: summary court-martial (minor offenses, no jail over 30 days), special court-martial (intermediate, up to one year confinement), and general court-martial (most serious charges, unlimited punishment including death). The accused has the right to trial by military judge alone or panel of officers (and enlisted members upon request). Convictions are reviewed through the military appellate system up to the Court of Appeals for the Armed Forces (CAAF).', 'military', 'Federal/Military', ['UCMJ', 'JAG Corps', 'Military Appeals'], ['UCMJ Art. 16-20', '10 U.S.C. §§ 816-820']],
  ['Servicemembers Civil Relief Act (SCRA)', 'A federal law that provides protections to active-duty military members in civil legal proceedings and financial obligations, including a cap on interest rates at 6% on pre-service debts, stays of civil proceedings, protections against default judgments and evictions, and the right to terminate certain leases and financial contracts. The SCRA recognizes that military service may impair service members\' ability to meet civilian obligations and protect their legal rights. Violations may result in civil liability and, in some cases, criminal penalties.', 'military', 'Federal', ['Veterans Benefits', 'Military Service', 'Civil Rights'], ['50 U.S.C. § 3901 et seq.', 'SCRA of 2003']],
  ['VA Disability Rating', 'A percentage assigned by the Department of Veterans Affairs reflecting the degree to which a service-connected disability impairs a veteran\'s earning capacity and quality of life, used to calculate monthly disability compensation benefits. Ratings range from 0% to 100% in increments of 10%. Combined ratings for multiple disabilities use the "whole person" method, not simple addition. Veterans may appeal rating decisions to the Board of Veterans Appeals, the Court of Appeals for Veterans Claims, and ultimately the Federal Circuit.', 'military', 'Federal', ['Veterans Benefits', 'VA Compensation', 'Service Connection'], ['38 U.S.C. § 1110', '38 C.F.R. § 4.1']],
  ['Discharge Upgrade', 'The administrative process by which a veteran with a discharge less than honorable (general, other than honorable, bad conduct, or dishonorable) may apply to the applicable Discharge Review Board or Board for Correction of Military Records to have the discharge character upgraded. Iraq and Afghanistan era veterans with trauma-related conditions may petition arguing PTSD, MST, or TBI impaired their conduct. A successful upgrade may restore eligibility for VA benefits otherwise unavailable to veterans with less-than-honorable discharges.', 'military', 'Federal', ['VA Benefits', 'UCMJ', 'Administrative Review'], ['10 U.S.C. § 1553', '32 C.F.R. §§ 70, 865']],
  ['Absent Without Leave (AWOL)', 'The military offense of being absent from duty without authorization, charged under Article 86 of the UCMJ. AWOL becomes desertion if the absence exceeds 30 days or if the service member intended to remain absent permanently or avoid hazardous duty. Punishment depends on the duration of absence and circumstances; in time of war, desertion may be punishable by death. AWOL service members may be returned to military control through apprehension, surrender, or civilian arrest.', 'military', 'Federal/Military', ['Court-Martial', 'UCMJ', 'Desertion'], ['UCMJ Art. 86', '10 U.S.C. § 886']],
  ['UNIFORMED SERVICES EMPLOYMENT AND REEMPLOYMENT RIGHTS ACT (USERRA)', 'A federal law protecting the job rights of individuals who voluntarily or involuntarily leave employment to undertake military service. USERRA prohibits discrimination based on military service in initial employment, reemployment, retention, promotion, and benefits. Service members who are absent for military duty are entitled to reemployment in the position they would have attained absent the absence (the "escalator principle"). The Department of Labor and, for federal employees, the Office of Special Counsel enforce USERRA.', 'military', 'Federal', ['Employment Law', 'Military Service', 'SCRA'], ['38 U.S.C. § 4301 et seq.', 'USERRA']],

  // ─── MARITIME / ADMIRALTY LAW (EXPANDED) ────────────────────────────────
  ['Maritime Lien', 'A privileged claim on a vessel created by operation of law for goods and services necessary for the ship\'s operation or for damages caused by the vessel, attaching to the ship itself and traveling with it regardless of changes in ownership. Maritime liens arise for necessaries (supplies, repairs, towage), seaman\'s wages, salvage, and tort claims from vessel collisions. Liens are enforced by arresting the vessel in an in rem admiralty action, and priority among competing liens is governed by federal maritime law.', 'maritime', 'Federal', ['Arrest of Vessel', 'Ship Mortgage', 'Necessaries'], ['46 U.S.C. § 31341', 'Commercial Instruments and Maritime Liens Act']],
  ['Arrest of Vessel', 'The seizure of a ship by judicial process to create security for a maritime claim, exercised through in rem admiralty jurisdiction by filing a complaint and directing the U.S. Marshal to take custody of the vessel. Federal courts have in rem admiralty jurisdiction over vessels in U.S. waters. The vessel owner may substitute security (a letter of undertaking or bond) to have the arrest vacated and the ship released. Rule C of the Supplemental Rules for Admiralty governs vessel arrest procedures.', 'maritime', 'Federal', ['Maritime Lien', 'In Rem Jurisdiction', 'Admiralty Jurisdiction'], ['Fed. R. Civ. P. Supp. Rule C', '28 U.S.C. § 1333']],
  ['Charter Party', 'A contract for the hire of a vessel or its cargo capacity, taking the form of a voyage charter (vessel hired for a specific voyage), time charter (vessel hired for a specific period), or bareboat/demise charter (bare vessel hire where the charterer assumes responsibility for crew and operation). The charter party defines freight rates, laytime, demurrage (charges for delayed loading/unloading), and the respective obligations of shipowner and charterer. Most charter parties are on standard forms (GENCON, NYPE) subject to negotiated amendments.', 'maritime', 'Federal/International', ['Bill of Lading', 'Demurrage', 'Freight'], ['GENCON Charter Party', 'NYPE Time Charter']],
  ['Bill of Lading', 'A document issued by a carrier to a shipper that serves simultaneously as a receipt for goods, a contract of carriage, and a negotiable document of title entitling the holder to receive the goods. The Carriage of Goods by Sea Act (COGSA) governs bills of lading for cargo shipped to or from U.S. ports, limiting carrier liability to $500 per package unless a higher value is declared. A "clean" bill of lading (no noted defects) is a prerequisite for payment under most letters of credit in international trade.', 'maritime', 'Federal/International', ['Charter Party', 'COGSA', 'Cargo Damage'], ['46 U.S.C. § 30701', 'Carriage of Goods by Sea Act (COGSA)']],
  ['Salvage', 'A maritime law right entitling a person who voluntarily and successfully rescues a ship or cargo from peril to an award from the property\'s owner, based on the value of the property saved, the degree of danger, the risk to the salvor and equipment, the success of the service, and the skill exhibited. Salvage awards are determined by admiralty courts. The International Convention on Salvage (1989) also recognizes "special compensation" for salvors who prevent environmental damage.', 'maritime', 'Federal/International', ['Maritime Lien', 'General Average', 'Admiralty Jurisdiction'], ['46 U.S.C. § 80107', 'International Convention on Salvage (1989)']],
  ['Longshore and Harbor Workers\' Compensation Act (LHWCA)', 'A federal workers\' compensation statute providing disability compensation, medical benefits, and survivor benefits to workers employed in maritime occupations on the navigable waters of the United States or in adjoining areas (piers, docks, terminals). The LHWCA covers longshore workers, ship repairers, shipbuilders, and shipbreakers. Claims are adjudicated by administrative law judges within the Department of Labor, with review by the Benefits Review Board and circuit courts.', 'maritime', 'Federal', ['Jones Act', 'Maritime Tort', 'Workers\' Compensation'], ['33 U.S.C. § 901 et seq.', 'LHWCA']],
  ['Oil Pollution Act of 1990 (OPA 90)', 'A federal statute enacted in response to the Exxon Valdez oil spill that establishes liability for the costs of oil spill cleanup and damages to natural resources, real and personal property, subsistence use, revenues, and profits. OPA 90 created a tiered liability system with responsible parties liable without fault up to statutory caps, with the Oil Spill Liability Trust Fund (OSLTF) compensating when caps are exceeded or the responsible party is unknown. The statute also mandated double-hull construction for oil tankers in U.S. waters.', 'maritime', 'Federal', ['Environmental Law', 'Maritime Liability', 'CERCLA'], ['33 U.S.C. § 2701 et seq.', 'OPA 90']],
  ['Seaman Status', 'A legal classification that determines eligibility for maritime remedies including maintenance and cure, the right to sue for unseaworthiness, and Jones Act negligence claims. A seaman must have a connection to a vessel or fleet of vessels in navigation that is substantial in both duration and nature, and contribute to the vessel\'s function or mission. The Supreme Court\'s Chandris v. Latsis (1995) set forth the controlling test, requiring an employee to spend roughly 30% of their work time in service of a vessel.', 'maritime', 'Federal', ['Jones Act', 'Maintenance and Cure', 'Unseaworthiness'], ['Chandris v. Latsis (1995)', '46 U.S.C. § 30104']],

  // ─── EDUCATION LAW (EXPANDED) ────────────────────────────────────────────
  ['Free Appropriate Public Education (FAPE)', 'The right of students with disabilities under the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA) to receive special education and related services that are designed to meet their unique needs and prepare them for further education, employment, and independent living, at no cost to parents. The Supreme Court in Endrew F. v. Douglas County School District (2017) held that FAPE requires an educational program "reasonably calculated to enable a child to make progress appropriate in light of the child\'s circumstances," setting a higher standard than de minimis benefit.', 'education', 'Federal', ['IEP', 'IDEA', 'Least Restrictive Environment'], ['20 U.S.C. § 1400 et seq.', 'Endrew F. v. Douglas County School Dist. (2017)']],
  ['Individualized Education Program (IEP)', 'A written document developed by a team including parents, teachers, and specialists that describes the special education and related services to be provided to a student with a disability under IDEA, including present levels of performance, annual goals, services, placement, and transition planning for students 16 and older. IEPs must be reviewed at least annually and the student reevaluated every three years. Parents who disagree with an IEP have procedural rights including the right to an independent educational evaluation and due process hearing.', 'education', 'Federal', ['FAPE', 'IDEA', 'Least Restrictive Environment'], ['20 U.S.C. § 1414(d)', 'IDEA (2004)']],
  ['Least Restrictive Environment (LRE)', 'The IDEA requirement that students with disabilities be educated with nondisabled peers to the maximum extent appropriate, and removed from the regular educational environment only when the nature or severity of the disability is such that education in regular classes cannot be achieved satisfactorily even with the use of supplementary aids and services. LRE is determined individually for each student in light of their IEP goals. Courts apply the Roncker portability test or the Rachel H. regular academic benefits test to assess LRE compliance.', 'education', 'Federal', ['IEP', 'FAPE', 'IDEA'], ['20 U.S.C. § 1412(a)(5)', 'Sacramento City Unified School Dist. v. Rachel H. (9th Cir. 1994)']],
  ['Clery Act', 'The Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, a federal law requiring colleges and universities receiving federal financial aid to maintain and disclose information about crime on and near their campuses, publish an annual security report, and issue timely warnings for ongoing threats. The Department of Education enforces the Clery Act. The Sexual Assault Victims\' Bill of Rights (1992) requires institutions to inform victims of their rights, and the Campus SaVE Act (2013) expanded Clery requirements to include stalking, dating violence, and domestic violence statistics.', 'education', 'Federal', ['Title IX', 'Campus Safety', 'Higher Education Act'], ['20 U.S.C. § 1092(f)', 'Clery Act']],
  ['Zero Tolerance Policy', 'School disciplinary policies mandating predetermined consequences, typically suspension or expulsion, for specified offenses such as weapons, drugs, or violent behavior, regardless of the circumstances, motivation, or severity of the individual incident. Zero tolerance policies gained prominence following the Gun-Free Schools Act of 1994. Critics argue they contribute to the "school-to-prison pipeline" and result in disproportionate discipline of minority students. Courts review disciplinary exclusions under due process standards established in Goss v. Lopez (1975).', 'education', 'Federal/State', ['Student Discipline', 'Due Process', 'IDEA'], ['20 U.S.C. § 7961', 'Goss v. Lopez (1975)']],
  ['School Search and Seizure', 'The constitutional standards governing searches of students and their belongings by school officials, governed by the Fourth Amendment standard of "reasonable suspicion" rather than probable cause as established in New Jersey v. T.L.O. (1985). A search is justified at its inception if there are reasonable grounds to believe it will turn up evidence of a violation of law or school rules; the search must also be reasonably related in scope to the circumstances. Safford Unified School District v. Redding (2009) held that strip search of a student required more individualized suspicion.', 'education', 'Federal', ['Fourth Amendment', 'Student Rights', 'Drug Testing'], ['New Jersey v. T.L.O. (1985)', 'Safford Unified School Dist. v. Redding (2009)']],
  ['IDEA Manifestation Determination Review', 'A required IDEA review triggered when a school seeks to suspend a student with a disability for more than 10 cumulative school days in a year, determining whether the conduct was caused by or substantially related to the student\'s disability, or was the direct result of the school\'s failure to implement the IEP. If the conduct is found to be a manifestation of the disability, the school may not discipline through change of placement and must conduct a functional behavioral assessment. If not a manifestation, the school may discipline the student in the same manner as nondisabled students.', 'education', 'Federal', ['IEP', 'IDEA', 'Student Discipline'], ['20 U.S.C. § 1415(k)', 'IDEA (2004)']],
];

module.exports = { terms };
